<p>The United States has lifted sanctions on Venezuela’s acting president, Delcy Rodríguez. In a move announced by the Treasury Department on April 1, Washington removed her from the Specially Designated Nationals list, allowing her to do business with U.S. entities, regain access to blocked assets and potentially travel to the United States. The step deepens Washington’s recognition of Rodríguez as Venezuela’s legitimate authority after the U.S. formally recognized her government following the capture of her predecessor, Nicolás Maduro. Several reports say the sanctions relief could bring the U.S. and Venezuela closer, including possible meetings in Washington and broader commercial ties, especially after Rodríguez complied with U.S. demands to open Venezuela’s energy sector to American companies. Overall, the decision marks a further shift away from pressure and isolation and toward direct engagement with Venezuela’s current leadership, though coverage differs in how outlets describe Maduro’s removal and the extent of U.S. influence in Caracas.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cx2Uv_xd2BQH9SHqYziAe123xwbRtuzUoaaSEN8ok4cAujWlAqiUonkRYxbVqlPhaZd0ovfQR2DBt3Nmf0yKbowa88fJpY0lqW5upCmMc2izA8AOFpEs1iGcemBe5J69AQH2sGLav92ypTIXwYd8j5KVpalo4yQkFgidinIAGjXm_G8SPKwHFLE2Tr7Bz4EwOMmS_RZf' alt='The Trump administration removed Delcy Rodríguez, the acting president of Venezuela, from its sanctions list on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Asset control: Removal from the SDN list lets Rodríguez regain access to blocked assets and legally do business with U.S. companies under U.S. financial rules.</li><li>Washington visit: The move clears a practical path for Rodríguez to travel to U.S. territory and could set up a meeting with President Trump, according to several reports.</li><li>Energy opening: Le Monde reported that ties warmed after Rodríguez complied with President Trump’s demand that Caracas open Venezuela’s energy industry to American companies.</li><li>Recognition signal: PBS and NPR described the sanctions relief as a strong sign that Washington now sees Rodríguez as Venezuela’s legitimate governing authority.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: The administration cast the sanctions removal as part of a broader effort to rebuild ties with Venezuela’s government while advancing formal recognition of Rodríguez’s authority. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-administration-lifts-sanctions-delcy-rodriguez-venezuela-acting-president/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Delcy Rodríguez: South China Morning Post reported that Rodríguez praised President Trump as Washington lifted sanctions and expanded its official recognition of her leadership. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/americas/article/3348729/us-lifts-sanctions-venezuelas-acting-president-rodriguez-who-praises-trump'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera: Al Jazeera framed the policy shift as evidence of growing U.S. influence over Venezuela’s government after Maduro’s removal, emphasizing the coercive side of Washington’s role. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/1/us-removes-sanctions-on-venezuelas-interim-president-delcy-rodriguez'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Deutsche Welle: DW described the move as part of a gradual easing of U.S. measures on Venezuela after Maduro was toppled, highlighting a broader normalization process rather than a single diplomatic gesture. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-lifts-sanctions-on-venezuela-s-acting-president-delcy-rodriguez/a-76635151'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/01/us/politics/us-lifts-sanctions-rodriguez.html'>U.S. Lifts Sanctions on Venezuela’s New Leader, Delcy Rodríguez</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/us-lifts-sanctions-on-venezuelas-interim-president'>US lifts sanctions on Venezuela acting President, opening door for assets control</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li>